WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.359
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:07.220
Thank you very much for joining us today.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.660
We couldn't imagine a better speaker for
this memorial lecture.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.180
We have all sorts of topics to
discuss from your investing career,

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.400
to your work on the environment,
to your recent political activism.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.540
Before we get there, I wanted to start
with your time as a GSB student.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.850
Back when you were in these seats,
metaphorically speaking,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:30.665
did you ever imagine you would be on this
stage as a view from the top speaker?

00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:35.710
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.090
&gt;&gt; I can't say I was thinking this far

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.470
forward, and I can't say

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:48.666
I really understood the significance when
I was going to Stanford Business School,

00:00:48.666 --> 00:00:52.540
to Stanford Business School,
in terms of leadership in our society.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:01:00.210
So, I think I have a different perspective
of the importance of this institution now.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.792
And obviously, I'm a lot older and

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:08.780
have [INAUDIBLE]
and wouldn't have time for anything.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.260
&gt;&gt; Fair enough.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.820
I mean, we're all facing the same
types of constraints here.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.785
Another question for you, what was your
most influential class at the GSB?

00:01:21.807 --> 00:01:25.815
&gt;&gt; So, I think I'd chase two classes,

00:01:25.815 --> 00:01:28.550
I'll equivocate.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.550
One was touchy feely.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:31.182
I don't know,

00:01:31.182 --> 00:01:33.350
do they still do interpersonal dynamics?
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:34.733
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:36.418
&gt;&gt; So, I love touchy feely,

00:01:36.418 --> 00:01:40.404
because it was a chance to see how
people interact in a much more

00:01:40.404 --> 00:01:44.250
honest way than when people
are normally very controlled.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.170
And also, when I went home for Christmas
break, and my father looked at me and

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.970
goes, is this a class, seriously?

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.511
So I love that too, just annoying him.

00:01:53.511 --> 00:01:58.688
But I also took a course here in
International Economics that was,

00:01:58.688 --> 00:02:03.495
I thought absolutely brilliant
in terms about thinking about

00:02:03.495 --> 00:02:07.849
basically competitive
advantage on a national level.

00:02:07.849 --> 00:02:13.786
And thinking about what makes societies
work, what makes societies productive,

00:02:13.786 --> 00:02:17.274
why do some societies
move forward really well?

00:02:17.274 --> 00:02:20.647
And what are the reasons that other
societies have continuing problems?

00:02:20.647 --> 00:02:26.340
And I thought that was something that
I never looked at so systematically.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.540
It was really a brilliant course.
&gt;&gt; Speaking of making societies

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.210
productive, let's now turn to
your early days as an investor.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:37.430
You were a rising star at Goldman
when Wall Street was booming, and

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.350
in less than a year,

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:43.060
you had moved to San Francisco to start-up
a small merger arbitrage hedge fund.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.554
What gave you the confidence
to make this sudden change?

00:02:48.797 --> 00:02:49.938
&gt;&gt; So let's be clear,

00:02:49.938 --> 00:02:53.023
when I moved back out here from Goldman,
first of all,

00:02:53.023 --> 00:02:57.230
my wife wanted to live west
of the Mississippi, period.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:00.410
So, I knew that Goldman Sachs
was in New York,

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.210
and therefore,
I was going to have to leave.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:06.373
It's also true that I got in a gigantic
fight with a guy I was working for,

00:03:06.373 --> 00:03:10.672
and therefore, I felt like, under no
circumstances do I want to continue to

00:03:10.672 --> 00:03:13.636
go to work and see that guy every morning.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:13.636 --> 00:03:17.200
&gt;&gt; Which was not a great career move.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:17.720
But also,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:23.850
I felt like one of the things I love about
investing is you can measure the results.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:28.460
And I felt like, if I do a bad job at
Goldman Sachs, they're going to fire me.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:32.990
If I do a bad job on my own,
I'm also going to get fired.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:34.665
But if I do a good job on my own,

00:03:34.665 --> 00:03:37.920
I'd have much more upside
then I do at Goldman Sachs.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.770
And so, I didn't think I was taking more
risk because I felt like, either way,

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:45.770
if you don't do a good job in life,
it doesn't work out.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:50.100
So, I was more than willing to
bet on myself in the sense that

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.740
if you're taking that risk anyway, why
not at least give yourself the up side?

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.250
&gt;&gt; And have the opportunity to go surfing

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.580
on weekends is helpful.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.192
&gt;&gt; We did want to live west of

00:04:00.192 --> 00:04:03.650
the Mississippi.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You then went

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.250
from $10 million in seed capital,

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.400
to managing about $20 billion by the time
you stepped down from Fairlawn in 2012.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.371
What challenges did you face as the fund
scaled, particularly those related to

00:04:15.371 --> 00:04:23.190
leadership?
&gt;&gt; First of all,

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:26.680
one of the things that we tried
to do was to grow gradually.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.560
Because I felt if you went from
$10 million to $20 million,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.240
that wasn't a step function,
so that you could

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:40.150
change your team slowly enough, so
that you continue to do the job.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.830
If you went from $10
million to $300 million,

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.790
you're in a completely different business,
and you're taking a gigantic risk,

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:51.430
with whether you have the capability
to actually perform that function.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:57.460
So, we really grew very consistently,
but we never grew geometrically.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:04.230
I will say this, when I think back
to what we were doing at Farallon,

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:09.090
and when I'm mad at myself, I think
that there's a rule in organizations

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:13.820
that up to 125 people, you don't
have to be that formally organized.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:18.600
And I remember being here in the HP wave,
management by walking around.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.470
That's what I really like to do,
was just talk to everybody, and try and

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:25.870
stay up to date, and
hear what the problems were.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:30.900
And we had a very open office
physically so that there were no walls.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.999
There were no offices, so that there
was a lot of internal communication,

00:05:34.999 --> 00:05:36.831
whether people wanted it or not.

00:05:36.831 --> 00:05:41.019
I think above 125 people,
you start to really need rules, and

00:05:41.019 --> 00:05:46.600
you really need hierarchy, and you
really need a way of organizing people.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.462
And we did do that.

00:05:48.462 --> 00:05:53.244
But if there's one thing that I would
fault myself for, and I do get mad about

00:05:53.244 --> 00:05:57.680
it is, I think at some point,
we had a very, very flat organization.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.948
And I really did feel like we made
decisions listening to everybody

00:06:02.948 --> 00:06:07.588
very clearly, and we didn't really
have a hierarchical decision making.

00:06:07.588 --> 00:06:11.643
And if I were doing it again, I would be a
little more insistent that the people who

00:06:11.643 --> 00:06:15.579
proved themselves, into which I would
throw my name, should get a little more

00:06:15.579 --> 00:06:19.458
decision making than some of the people
who thought they knew a heck of a lot and

00:06:19.458 --> 00:06:22.715
then would be like, my gosh,
I had no idea that would happen.

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:26.394
Usually I'm like, well,
that's what I was talking to you about for

00:06:26.394 --> 00:06:27.541
the last six months.

00:06:27.541 --> 00:06:32.608
And so, I, there is, when I think back
to growth as you get more larger,

00:06:32.608 --> 00:06:35.847
you actually have to
able to have some system,

00:06:35.847 --> 00:06:41.552
where the senior people really do have
to listen, have to take into account.

00:06:41.552 --> 00:06:45.400
But ultimately, they're supposed to be
in the position that they're going to

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.370
get hung if it doesn't turn
out to be a good decision,

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:51.300
so the younger people
should defer to them,

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.830
until they can get in that position.
&gt;&gt; As you were growing consistently, one

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.430
thing that was also growing was Fairlawn's
investments in the coal industry.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:04.220
Fairlawn became one of the major
players in sustaining and

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.710
growing the coal industry in Australia,
Indonesia, and China.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.570
Knowing what you know now, in a different
time, would you have made those

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.065
investments again?
&gt;&gt; No.

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:15.210
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.980
&gt;&gt; Look, I would say, somewhere around

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:24.020
ten years ago,
basically I was sitting here

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:29.010
thinking about what was going to
turn out to be a huge problem.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.730
And I started to really focus on climate,
and

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:34.080
then I started to look at all
our investments differently.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:40.876
And so, it really was a situation
where I felt like up till then,

00:07:40.876 --> 00:07:46.190
we invested in every single industry
including fossil fuels, which were

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:50.540
probably less than proportionate to what
they are in the whole worldwide economy,

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:54.380
or the American economy, but
definitely significant investments.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.200
And starting about ten years ago,
I realized,

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.930
wow, this is just going
to be a gigantic problem.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:04.750
And I've gotta not only stop doing this,
but I've gotta get out of this,

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.660
which I did do, and have done.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.064
But I feel like, do I wish that I'd
come to that conclusion 10 years or 20?

00:08:12.064 --> 00:08:13.490
Of course I do.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:17.095
Do I wish I were taller and
playing center in the NBA, yes.

00:08:17.095 --> 00:08:17.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:19.090
&gt;&gt; I wish I were smarter and

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.550
had figured it out sooner.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:25.580
But I think that we're really coming from
as a society is exactly the same place,

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:26.540
which is this.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:30.610
Our society was based
around fossil fuel energy.

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:31.430
That's just the truth.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:36.150
We all drive cars, we've all grown up
in a society where electricity was

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.779
largely based on fossil fuels.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.190
It's not a question of that.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.280
The question is what are we
going to do about it, and

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.210
how fast can we move away from it, and
how fast do we have to move away from it?

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:51.130
So of course I wish that when my good
friend Dan Lashof was writing his PhD

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:56.650
thesis on climate change in 1989,
that I was like Dan's absolutely right.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.165
We should be really careful about this.

00:08:58.165 --> 00:09:03.673
But it took me longer than,
obviously, the sooner the better,

00:09:03.673 --> 00:09:08.588
but I feel like ten years ago,
I came to that conclusion.

00:09:08.588 --> 00:09:13.380
And I decided this is really important,
that this is a thing that

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:18.650
will be critical for
our society and critical for

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:23.140
everyone on this planet, so therefore,
we can't equivocate about it,

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:26.060
wish I'd been smarter.
&gt;&gt; Could we drill down on that

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:26.760
moment a little?

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.830
Because I think there's a very deep
amount of thought that you put into this

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:33.470
particular issue, and yet, there's so
many issues out there in the world.

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.080
And you've spoken about many,
you've spoken about inequality, and

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.570
you've spoken about tobacco, and
the harms that it poses to society.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.760
You've gone through many different topics.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.960
How is it that climate resonated so
deeply?

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:45.790
And specifically,

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.222
what you saw at that time ten years ago?
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:09:48.222 --> 00:09:50.771
let me give you my thinking then,
which is this.

00:09:50.771 --> 00:09:57.047
I mean, I know the GSPS has a ton
of foreign born students, but

00:09:57.047 --> 00:10:02.859
as an American,
I felt like our system basically works.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:08.530
And the way it basically works
is we're rude, we're vulgar,

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:11.180
we're loud, we disagree with each other.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:14.920
But if everybody's trying to solve
the problem, regardless of where they're

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.560
coming from, we compromise,
we solve the problem, we move on.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.710
And everyone else is thinking
they're very rude and loud,

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:23.880
which is true, but we compromise and
we solve the problem.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:30.393
And I looked at climate and I thought
my gosh, we're not solving the problem,

00:10:30.393 --> 00:10:35.346
that we have this huge problem that
could be existential for a society.

00:10:35.346 --> 00:10:36.894
And for some reason,

00:10:36.894 --> 00:10:43.180
the normal American loud conflictual
compromise isn't happening here.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.010
And I felt like I really
believe in our system, so

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:49.850
I want to get into our
system more to try and

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:55.460
figure out how I can be part of its
normal solution for big problems for us.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:00.690
And so, I have changed,
actually, my understanding

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:05.910
as a result of getting into that question,
and diving into that problem specifically.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:09.730
It's changed my understanding
of how America really works.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:13.230
But the reason I did that problem
was I though it was existential, and

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:18.580
I didn't understand why the normal
American, democratic, ugly, messy,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:23.060
loud way wasn't working in that case.
&gt;&gt; This gives us a really

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.290
good transition over to your
extensive philanthropic work.

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:28.770
You signed onto the giving pledge in 2010.

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.000
And you've been especially active
in environmental causes, especially

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.320
since that moment that you described
where you saw the light, so to speak.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.970
How did you go about finding
the right causes to back?

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:40.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] By,

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:44.876
I mean, it's such a classic thing.

00:11:44.876 --> 00:11:49.260
I think that you learn a lot by doing,
and I think that what we've found

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:53.920
over time is, hopefully,
we're a lot better at what we're doing,

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:57.460
as a result of all the things that
we did that weren't so smart.

00:11:57.460 --> 00:12:02.213
And, to a large extent,
I would say, my understanding of

00:12:02.213 --> 00:12:06.772
the idea of philanthropy is
really different from what I

00:12:06.772 --> 00:12:11.726
would say is a traditional
understanding of philanthropy.

00:12:11.726 --> 00:12:13.064
Because actually,

00:12:13.064 --> 00:12:17.940
what we've tried to do is to set up
operating enterprises in the real world

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:23.760
that are hands on to actually affect
the kinds of things we're hoping for.

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.120
It sounds complicated.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:32.270
But for instance, we have a community bank
where the equity is through a foundation.

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.150
So we can never get the money back.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:37.230
So in a way, we've given
the money away forever and ever.

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.330
But it's a bank that's FDIC insured.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:43.460
It makes loans for economic justice,

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:47.540
environmental sustainability,
women in minority own businesses.

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:53.810
So it's got a very specific
social thrust done through our

00:12:53.810 --> 00:13:00.160
capitalist economic system, but
with a complete value driven agenda.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:06.950
So it's kind of, we're not giving money
away to people, we're lending money.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.660
We need them to pay us back with interest,
so

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.650
we can make more loans to other people,
so they can succeed.

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:16.380
And so that's not, I mean, I've never
heard of someone else doing that,

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.270
or thinking of that as philanthropy.

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:20.940
In fact, when we suggest to other
people that it's a good idea,

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:24.810
they look very askance, but

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:29.850
we really believe in operating
enterprises where people are involved,

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.080
really in the real world trying
to have impact in a big way, and

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:38.990
also to show that things are possible.
&gt;&gt; Is that a strategy that underlies

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:43.018
your focus on both the Center and
the Tomcat Center here at Stanford?

00:13:43.018 --> 00:13:46.765
I mean, you clearly believe that
Stanford has a role to play in

00:13:46.765 --> 00:13:51.585
your strategy in fighting climate change.
&gt;&gt; So, one of the things that's true,

00:13:51.585 --> 00:13:54.828
if you look at climate, and
you kind of divide it into what

00:13:54.828 --> 00:13:59.037
is happening in the real world in terms
of the natural world changes, and

00:13:59.037 --> 00:14:02.694
what's happening in terms of research,
and technology, and

00:14:02.694 --> 00:14:07.190
capability of dealing with the problems or
solving the problems.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:11.330
And then our willingness to actually solve
the problems and use the technology and

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:15.540
the techniques that we have I felt as if,
and I still feel like,

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:22.080
I mean, I love Stanford, Stanford
has led revolutions in technology.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:27.204
I wanted Stanford to use its
broad range of capabilities,

00:14:27.204 --> 00:14:30.680
to be a leader in this on every scale.

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:34.610
So from an investment side,
a legal side, a technology side,

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:41.540
I felt like Stanford has done this in IT,
why shouldn't we be doing this in energy?

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:47.290
There are very few places that have
the breadth of research, the sort

00:14:47.290 --> 00:14:52.820
of capability of across the board making
this difference, and making it happen.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:57.810
And so, part of the solution to
this is our ability to solve

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:02.670
it from a technical basis, and then to
understand how that fits into society.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.540
And so, since I love Stanford, and
since I knew the capabilities,

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:12.680
I felt like I would really like
it if Stanford leads on this.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:16.840
And if you actually look at what
Stanford did on renewing its

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:22.230
own electricity plant for
the campus, that was, I think,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:27.690
designated the best engineering project
in the United States of America in 2015.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:35.220
The Stanford electricity system,
so that we have the capability

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:40.220
of doing this in a way that is
significant and replicable, and

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:42.936
that's what I wanted to see.
&gt;&gt; Apparently,

00:15:42.936 --> 00:15:45.944
we're also pretty good at football.

00:15:45.944 --> 00:15:50.626
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Wow, where I goeth before a fall.

00:15:50.626 --> 00:15:51.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:53.507
&gt;&gt; We'll pass on the message to

00:15:53.507 --> 00:15:55.726
David Shaw.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:55.726 --> 00:15:58.023
&gt;&gt; But before we go to national politics,

00:15:58.023 --> 00:16:02.460
I'd like to focus a bit on your
activism here in our own backyard.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.830
In California,

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:08.990
voter ballot initiatives allow the public
to vote directly on proposed laws.

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:13.300
You have focused on these ballot
initiatives as a way to influence a wide

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:17.560
range of policies, including climate and
energy legislation.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.906
How did you arrive at
this particular approach?

00:16:22.948 --> 00:16:25.525
&gt;&gt; So for the people who aren't California

00:16:25.525 --> 00:16:28.328
citizens, we do have
this extremely fat and

00:16:28.328 --> 00:16:33.840
complicated ballot where you're basically
allowed to do direct democracy.

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.950
So if you can get enough
signatures raised,

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:40.706
you can put a proposition on the ballot
that everybody gets to vote on.

00:16:40.706 --> 00:16:45.310
And if 50.1% of the people vote for
it, you have a new law.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:49.540
And this has led to some
truly heinous outcomes, and

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:51.850
it's also led to some
really good outcomes.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:57.640
And so, the first thing that we did,
was I co-chaired with George Schultz.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.820
Fighting a ballot initiative
which was designed to get

00:17:00.820 --> 00:17:03.070
rid of our progressive energy legislation.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:07.290
Our cap and trade system, and a bunch
of other things that have to do with

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:10.450
basically having the most progressive
energy laws in the world.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:11.860
That was in 2010,

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:16.740
two oil companies wanted to basically
change those laws and get rid of them.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.430
And so we were the No on 23 campaign.

00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:25.123
So why did we, we did that because
traditionally, when you get to

00:17:25.123 --> 00:17:30.169
the ballot and it's a question of
business versus environmentalist,

00:17:30.169 --> 00:17:34.556
jobs versus the environment,
jobs and business always win.

00:17:34.556 --> 00:17:37.656
And that was the way people thought
this was going to happen and

00:17:37.656 --> 00:17:40.720
there had been a previous
ballot initiative in 2006.

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:45.356
So four years before, where an individual
from L.A spent 70 million bucks and

00:17:45.356 --> 00:17:47.210
lost his initiative.

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:52.120
So there was no one rushing to be the
environmental side of this proposition,

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.710
so it's like most of the jobs I've had.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:56.840
There was no one else
stupid enough to take it.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.070
But the difference was,
it actually was a formative experience for

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:06.150
me because it really was about
climate in some ways but

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:08.220
we had a rule,
no one can talk about climate.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:11.810
The way that we're going to talk to people

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:15.780
is about local human issues of jobs and
clean air.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:17.070
And in fact, by far,

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:21.970
the best TV commercial we had was a
commercial of a woman who was the head in

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:25.720
California of the American Lung
Association talking about clean air and

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:28.650
the American Lung Association so
obviously she cared about it.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:34.578
She was very credible, a lovely person,
came across incredibly well and

00:18:34.578 --> 00:18:39.072
we got 70% of the vote for
people who said basically,

00:18:39.072 --> 00:18:44.260
we don't want to get rid of our
clean air/climate legislation.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:49.570
We also found, and I think it was
incredibly significant for me,

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:54.380
that we wanted a different message
which was more jobs, cleaner air.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:57.290
But we also wanted a completely
different corelation.

00:18:57.290 --> 00:19:01.572
So we spent, we knew that all the people
who are traditional environmentalists,

00:19:01.572 --> 00:19:05.620
of course,
would be on our side in terms of groups.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:10.680
The NRDC, EDF,
League of Conservation Voters,

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.260
Sierra Club, of course,
those people would be for us.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:16.560
But actually when you look
at the people of California,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:20.930
the number one group who supports
everything environmental, everything about

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:25.045
progressive energy, everything
against climate change, are Latinos.

00:19:25.045 --> 00:19:29.910
Number two group, African Americans,
number three group Asian Americans.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:32.630
So when we were looking at
the people of California for

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:37.680
votes, we knew from the beginning we were
going to get progressive business and

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:43.070
we basically split the chambers
of commerce around the state.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:46.270
We had at least half
the chambers on our side.

00:19:46.270 --> 00:19:49.933
We had organized labor but
went to every community group and

00:19:49.933 --> 00:19:53.019
we wanted to make sure
that people understood it.

00:19:53.019 --> 00:19:58.361
Because actually, the people who
are not necessarily considered members

00:19:58.361 --> 00:20:03.840
of the CR club and Mike Brunes once
tried to kill me for saying this.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.587
But people who haven't necessarily been
the, what you think of as the fabled

00:20:08.587 --> 00:20:12.253
environmentalists actually
are the environmentalists.

00:20:12.253 --> 00:20:16.414
And that's how we got 70% of the vote
because we looked at a completely

00:20:16.414 --> 00:20:21.305
different slice of Californians, and
we said our coalition is much broader and

00:20:21.305 --> 00:20:23.630
our message is much different.

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:27.640
And that is something that
I learned in that year and

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:32.510
it was true then and it's true now.
&gt;&gt; So

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:37.262
these campaigns you've organized on
behalf of these clauses are not cheap.

00:20:37.262 --> 00:20:42.240
[LAUGH] California's all ready one of
the most expensive media markets in

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:45.440
the country and
as these things go national,

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.280
I mean the costs grow
almost exponentially.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:52.220
What would you say to those who argue
that spending massive amounts of money on

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:56.373
these types of campaigns actually
undermines our democratic process?

00:20:56.373 --> 00:20:58.980
&gt;&gt; There's no question that the money in

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:01.260
politics is completely out of control and

00:21:01.260 --> 00:21:05.170
I would be strongly in favor
of changing the rules.

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:10.330
I completely agree with that, I do think
the money in politics is a cancer and

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:15.420
it's distorting and has led to some
terrible outcomes and continues to.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.350
You really can't look at
this tax bill that's going

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:22.600
through the Congress right now and not
see that that's exactly what's happening.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:26.590
That we have a fight between
monied interests and the people of

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:29.980
the United States, and the people of
the United States are losing badly.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:30.660
No question about it.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.070
That happens to be the system we have.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:40.634
So I could say, you know what,
I wish the system were different and

00:21:40.634 --> 00:21:43.870
until the system is different,
I'm going to sit on the sidelines.

00:21:43.870 --> 00:21:47.300
Or I could say, I really think
it's important that we keep our

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:52.150
clean energy legislation, I really think
it's important to close this tax loophole,

00:21:52.150 --> 00:21:55.760
I really think it's important to
stop young people from smoking and

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:58.260
just suck it up and spend the money.

00:21:58.260 --> 00:22:01.690
Or I could be pure and
say I'm going to keep the money and

00:22:01.690 --> 00:22:06.360
I'm going to wait until the system is
perfect which will be, by the way, never.

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:09.760
So I do think we should change it.

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:11.950
I'm completely against
Citizens United decision.

00:22:11.950 --> 00:22:16.060
I think it's an absolutely
terrible decision for democracy.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:20.680
I think it is distorting
the way our society is run.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:25.280
And I'm completely committed to
a change to what I would think of.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:26.750
If you look at what we do,

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:31.740
I believe the solution to our
problems is broader democracy and

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:34.995
broader participation and more power
to the people of the United States.

00:22:34.995 --> 00:22:40.200
So if you look at what our organization
does, we're a grassroots organization.

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:45.250
Last year, with our partners, we knocked
on 12 and a half million doors, we were

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:50.900
on 370 college campuses, we registered
807,000 people in the State of California.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:51.680
About 350,000 outside.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:56.100
Every one of those was an attempt to
get people who are under represented

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:01.760
to be registered, engaged,
and to participate.

00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:04.880
Because unless you are doing that,
your voice isn't heard and

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:07.224
you are not going to get a fair shake.

00:23:07.224 --> 00:23:11.740
So, everything we are doing
is about broader democracy,

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:15.640
more participation by people
who are underrepresented so

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.410
that they will get a fair
shake in our democracy.

00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:21.686
And the money side of this,
on the other side, is huge and

00:23:21.686 --> 00:23:25.046
absolutely contrary to every
single thing we're doing.

00:23:25.046 --> 00:23:28.982
And in fact,
they're fighting their head off to try and

00:23:28.982 --> 00:23:33.676
prevent Americans from being able
to exercise their franchise and

00:23:33.676 --> 00:23:37.520
to participate fully.
&gt;&gt; So I'm hearing you say something very

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:42.060
interesting which is that,
even if Gandhi once said, an eye for

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:45.790
an eye will make the world blind, you're
essentially telling us well, in this case,

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:46.970
two wrongs do make a right.

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.310
That if you want to fight,
you have to fight the fight with your own,

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:54.395
with whatever you've got on your arsenal.
&gt;&gt; No, no, no, no.

00:23:54.395 --> 00:23:56.405
I will not accept that question.

00:23:56.405 --> 00:23:59.462
I am not jabbing anyone's eye out.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:59.462 --> 00:24:01.355
&gt;&gt; That is absolutely unfair.

00:24:01.355 --> 00:24:04.367
I believe what they are doing
is anti-democratic And

00:24:04.367 --> 00:24:08.666
I believe the only thing I'm doing is
to try and make the democracy broader,

00:24:08.666 --> 00:24:10.491
more complete, and more just.

00:24:10.491 --> 00:24:12.201
I don't think this is an eye for an eye.

00:24:12.201 --> 00:24:16.653
I don't think I'm doing anything
that is not absolutely positive and

00:24:16.653 --> 00:24:20.284
in favor of the American system and
the American people.

00:24:20.284 --> 00:24:22.418
I'm not doing anything to hurt them.

00:24:22.418 --> 00:24:26.784
All I'm trying to do is have
a counterbalance supporting the people of

00:24:26.784 --> 00:24:28.046
the United States.

00:24:28.046 --> 00:24:30.009
This isn't an eye for an eye.

00:24:30.009 --> 00:24:31.449
This is completely different.

00:24:31.449 --> 00:24:37.157
This is an attempt to protect a system
that was set up over 200 years ago,

00:24:37.157 --> 00:24:42.140
where the idea is everybody is equal,
not every dollar is equal,

00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:45.853
but every person is equal.
&gt;&gt; So, and I happen, by the way,

00:24:45.853 --> 00:24:47.907
to support a lot of the causes you back.

00:24:47.907 --> 00:24:50.205
So, understand this comes
from a really good place.

00:24:50.205 --> 00:24:51.788
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:51.788 --> 00:24:52.965
&gt;&gt; Wait, George, trust me.

00:24:52.965 --> 00:24:56.605
If I disagree with you, I intend to.
&gt;&gt; I'm thinking it would be edifying for

00:24:56.605 --> 00:24:58.019
people to understand something.

00:24:58.019 --> 00:25:00.118
Which is you've built yourself,
occasionally,

00:25:00.118 --> 00:25:03.307
as the left's answer to the Koch Brothers.
&gt;&gt; Never, not one time.

00:25:03.307 --> 00:25:04.522
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:04.522 --> 00:25:05.421
&gt;&gt; Thank you for saying it.

00:25:05.421 --> 00:25:09.191
Not once, and never will.
&gt;&gt; Well, I've read it.

00:25:09.191 --> 00:25:09.995
&gt;&gt; Someone else has.

00:25:09.995 --> 00:25:11.225
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:11.225 --> 00:25:13.177
&gt;&gt; I promise, it's never happened.

00:25:13.177 --> 00:25:18.494
Other people ask me if I'm the left
answer to the Koch Brothers and

00:25:18.494 --> 00:25:20.717
I always say no.
&gt;&gt; All right well,

00:25:20.717 --> 00:25:24.627
well challenge accepted I guess.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:25:24.627 --> 00:25:25.712
&gt;&gt; I've been there every time.

00:25:25.712 --> 00:25:26.434
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:25:26.434 --> 00:25:28.977
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I agree that it's not a fair

00:25:28.977 --> 00:25:32.580
characterization to
compare people like that.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:36.374
But I think one thing that I was curious
to understand is what do you think

00:25:36.374 --> 00:25:40.296
the American left, or rather,
just progressive causes in general, and

00:25:40.296 --> 00:25:43.417
those who back them,
could learn from the Koch Brothers?

00:25:48.873 --> 00:25:50.298
&gt;&gt; Well, I'll say this,

00:25:50.298 --> 00:25:54.090
I mean I don't know if you guys,
have you guys read Dark Money?

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.163
There's a book about
the Koch Brothers essentially,

00:25:57.163 --> 00:26:01.505
kind of going back through their family
history about what they've done from

00:26:01.505 --> 00:26:05.072
an economic basis and
what they've done on a political basis.

00:26:05.072 --> 00:26:07.924
And they have been really consistent.

00:26:07.924 --> 00:26:14.881
They have been pushing on this stuff
really for over a century as a family.

00:26:14.881 --> 00:26:20.009
And so, I admire that fact that
they have continued to push on

00:26:20.009 --> 00:26:25.356
the things that they care about
over a very long period of time.

00:26:25.356 --> 00:26:29.316
And when you've said that, you've said
every single that I appreciate about

00:26:29.316 --> 00:26:31.969
them.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] We're talking about what can we

00:26:31.969 --> 00:26:33.048
learn from them.

00:26:33.048 --> 00:26:34.493
And we don't have to appreciate them.
&gt;&gt; Be

00:26:34.493 --> 00:26:35.801
consistent.
&gt;&gt; Be consistent.

00:26:35.801 --> 00:26:37.558
&gt;&gt; Keep trying.

00:26:37.558 --> 00:26:42.016
I think there is no question
that our organization,

00:26:42.016 --> 00:26:44.548
I quit my job five years ago and

00:26:44.548 --> 00:26:49.737
our organization has been up for
approximately five years.

00:26:49.737 --> 00:26:52.460
We're much better today than
we were five years ago.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:56.795
What we can do in terms
of going door to door,

00:26:56.795 --> 00:27:00.323
what we can do on college campuses.

00:27:00.323 --> 00:27:04.704
Our capability of making change
is much better because of

00:27:04.704 --> 00:27:09.004
the five years that we've
spent improving ourselves.

00:27:09.004 --> 00:27:13.289
I mean when we, I thought at Fairlawn,
and I think today,

00:27:13.289 --> 00:27:18.026
when I look back at 2017 I'll
say what did we do in 2017?

00:27:18.026 --> 00:27:18.583
Did it work?

00:27:18.583 --> 00:27:19.643
Didn't it work?

00:27:19.643 --> 00:27:23.995
I will also ask is our organization
stronger at the end of the year than

00:27:23.995 --> 00:27:26.180
it is at the beginning of the year?

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:30.635
Because there are lots of things you
can do that makes a year be good at

00:27:30.635 --> 00:27:35.106
the expense of a long term growth
of your organization capability.

00:27:35.106 --> 00:27:38.791
And I always feel like you
want to have good results and

00:27:38.791 --> 00:27:41.153
you want to grow your capability.

00:27:41.153 --> 00:27:43.991
And so when I think about
what we're trying to do and

00:27:43.991 --> 00:27:48.427
what I think progressives should be trying
to do is be aware of that second part.

00:27:48.427 --> 00:27:53.745
It's a long-term proposition
to get this country

00:27:53.745 --> 00:27:58.330
back on a trajectory of growth and
justice.

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:05.495
And so any one year, any one election
is not going to do it, we've always

00:28:05.495 --> 00:28:09.872
gotta be building capability overtime.
&gt;&gt; So

00:28:09.872 --> 00:28:13.998
speaking about building capability,
I think it's probably time just to change

00:28:13.998 --> 00:28:16.984
topics slightly to one that's
been in the news recently.

00:28:16.984 --> 00:28:22.765
I believe I've seen a commercial or two
regarding your love of President Trump.

00:28:22.765 --> 00:28:25.403
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:25.403 --> 00:28:27.557
&gt;&gt; If Trump invited you to work on climate

00:28:27.557 --> 00:28:30.595
change policy at the White House,
would you accept?

00:28:32.404 --> 00:28:36.080
&gt;&gt; No, there's absolutely no chance that

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:42.686
the president has any interest in
seriously working on climate policy.

00:28:42.686 --> 00:28:46.718
Every single thing he's done,
in the election and

00:28:46.718 --> 00:28:52.721
subsequent to the election has been to
work against us having progressive,

00:28:52.721 --> 00:28:55.929
objectively derived energy policies.

00:28:55.929 --> 00:29:01.691
So there is no chance that he would make
a good faith effort to have me come and

00:29:01.691 --> 00:29:04.068
try and come up with a program so

00:29:04.068 --> 00:29:08.471
that as a country we could move
forward in a positive way.

00:29:08.471 --> 00:29:13.655
And I think that the fact that
I'm saying that, I deeply regret,

00:29:13.655 --> 00:29:18.649
I started by saying my belief in
the American system is as long as

00:29:18.649 --> 00:29:22.985
you seed to the other person
that they're patriotic,

00:29:22.985 --> 00:29:27.528
that they're first goal is
to help the American people.

00:29:27.528 --> 00:29:35.038
And that they're driven by a belief
in data objectivity and analysis.

00:29:35.038 --> 00:29:36.023
That's the American system.

00:29:36.023 --> 00:29:39.539
And if people disagree with you fine,
of course they do.

00:29:39.539 --> 00:29:44.141
I mean anyone who's arrogant enough to
think they're always right is really

00:29:44.141 --> 00:29:45.004
ridiculous.

00:29:45.004 --> 00:29:49.747
So the American system is we may disagree
but as long as you seed good intentions,

00:29:49.747 --> 00:29:54.700
then we can work together, and compromise
in the middle, and probably come out with

00:29:54.700 --> 00:29:59.066
something better than either party
had at the start of the conversation.

00:29:59.066 --> 00:30:00.688
That's my basic belief.

00:30:00.688 --> 00:30:01.738
I don't believe for

00:30:01.738 --> 00:30:06.063
a second that those are the circumstances
under which that proposal would be made,

00:30:06.063 --> 00:30:08.077
and therefore, I wouldn't do it.
&gt;&gt; So

00:30:08.077 --> 00:30:12.935
in talking about growing the capability
of your organization, how would you say

00:30:12.935 --> 00:30:17.580
this particular campaign to impeach
President Trump grows the capability of

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:22.499
your organization over the long term?
&gt;&gt; So

00:30:22.499 --> 00:30:28.951
what we're actually doing,
which I encourage you to sign onto please,

00:30:28.951 --> 00:30:34.241
www.needtoimpeach.com, is
trying to give the American

00:30:34.241 --> 00:30:38.491
people directly a voice
in this conversation.

00:30:38.491 --> 00:30:43.111
And I think it's fair to say,
that the Republicans in Washington D.C.

00:30:43.111 --> 00:30:48.052
don't like it and the Democrats in
Washington, D.C. also don't like it.

00:30:48.052 --> 00:30:52.237
But the fact of the matter is
we have the technology for

00:30:52.237 --> 00:30:56.803
the American people to act
independently and to speak up.

00:30:56.803 --> 00:31:02.726
And the voice of the American people in
a democracy is supposed to be dispositive.

00:31:02.726 --> 00:31:07.682
And so what we're trying to do is give
Americans an easy way together to

00:31:07.682 --> 00:31:12.638
raise their voice because we believe
that we're actually in danger.

00:31:12.638 --> 00:31:17.522
That every single day the health and
safety of American citizens is in danger

00:31:17.522 --> 00:31:21.430
and that we can't sit by and
pretend that its not happening.

00:31:21.430 --> 00:31:24.116
And so when I think about this campaign,

00:31:24.116 --> 00:31:29.178
I think about it the same way you ask
me the questions about the proposition.

00:31:29.178 --> 00:31:31.746
The propositions are there for

00:31:31.746 --> 00:31:36.189
when the government in
Sacramento fails to be able for

00:31:36.189 --> 00:31:41.144
the people of California to be
able to enact laws together.

00:31:41.144 --> 00:31:45.199
And what we're doing with this
impeachment petition is to try and

00:31:45.199 --> 00:31:48.305
give the American people
a chance to do just that.

00:31:48.305 --> 00:31:52.345
Even though we can't make laws,
we can, in effect, raise our voice and

00:31:52.345 --> 00:31:54.268
change the conversation and say,

00:31:54.268 --> 00:31:57.931
there is something desperately
wrong here in the United States.

00:31:57.931 --> 00:32:00.111
This is not a partisan appeal.

00:32:00.111 --> 00:32:03.696
This is not to Democrats, or
Independents, or Republicans.

00:32:03.696 --> 00:32:06.779
This is to everybody who's worried
about the United States of America, and

00:32:06.779 --> 00:32:08.020
that is what we are trying to do.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.430
I had a look for the privacy policy
on the impeachment campaign, and

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:15.310
one of the things it says is that
the information that you provide can be

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:17.450
used for, essentially, future outreach.

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:19.891
Is that another objective
of this campaign?

00:32:19.891 --> 00:32:24.877
Is that, yes, maybe this campaign raises
awareness to some issues, but does it also

00:32:24.877 --> 00:32:29.241
provide a platform for a really devoted
set of followers to be reached out to

00:32:29.241 --> 00:32:34.048
over the course of your next objectives?
&gt;&gt; Well I

00:32:34.048 --> 00:32:38.625
would love it if we can get a way for
the American people

00:32:38.625 --> 00:32:43.406
to have their voice be heard
over a long period of time and

00:32:43.406 --> 00:32:48.021
also to provide a place to
basically put out content.

00:32:48.021 --> 00:32:53.414
So as far as I'm concerned,
look, we have seen information

00:32:53.414 --> 00:32:59.460
technology change the way people
relate in so many different ways.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:05.048
And the capability of Americans,
we've seen this all over the world.

00:33:05.048 --> 00:33:10.095
I mean, we have seen politics
turned upside-down by the ability

00:33:10.095 --> 00:33:14.420
of citizens around the world
to communicate real time.

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:20.121
And we've seen it here too,
in the election process.

00:33:20.121 --> 00:33:24.278
The question is, is there a way for
Americans to come together in a way so

00:33:24.278 --> 00:33:28.994
that we can actually have our voice be
heard when there's something desperately

00:33:28.994 --> 00:33:29.500
wrong?

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:32.930
That's what we're trying to do.
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much.

00:33:32.930 --> 00:33:37.071
One more question before we turn it
over to the audience for Q and A.

00:33:37.071 --> 00:33:41.840
I wanted just to focus back on the GSP
student body for a second and ask you.

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:46.928
If you could be a GSP student again,
and we'd be very fortunate to

00:33:46.928 --> 00:33:52.107
have you in our student body, and
wanted to help the environment,

00:33:52.107 --> 00:33:55.342
where would you go right now, and why?
&gt;&gt; As

00:33:55.342 --> 00:33:56.683
a job?
&gt;&gt; Yes, for

00:33:56.683 --> 00:34:02.055
a career.
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:34:02.055 --> 00:34:05.745
I'll say this, I don't think there's
one answer to that question.

00:34:05.745 --> 00:34:11.110
I have four kids who are 23,
25, 27, and 29.

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:17.481
I think that their generation, so for

00:34:17.481 --> 00:34:21.670
everybody who's sitting here
who's in that age range,

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:27.168
I think that your generation actually
is much more thoughtful about the way

00:34:27.168 --> 00:34:32.491
that you're thinking about your careers
and your lives than the class of

00:34:32.491 --> 00:34:37.586
1983 at the business school where
basically we would like a job.

00:34:37.586 --> 00:34:42.136
I think people now are much more proactive
in thinking about what getting that

00:34:42.136 --> 00:34:47.310
job will mean and what the implications
are down the line, I really do.

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:52.585
But I think when I look at my four kids,
my oldest son is working

00:34:52.585 --> 00:34:57.160
on basically software to
put renewables on the grid.

00:34:57.160 --> 00:35:02.007
My second son is an architecture student
who is trying to figure out how to use

00:35:02.007 --> 00:35:06.810
the mimicry of nature to make better
spaces for people to live in.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:11.256
My daughter is working at an impact
investor trying to get good returns, but

00:35:11.256 --> 00:35:15.916
also take into account what will mean for
the world if you make the investments.

00:35:15.916 --> 00:35:19.836
My youngest son is claims to be
about to take the MCATs, and

00:35:19.836 --> 00:35:26.010
he's working on health policy for
a Congressperson from Southern California.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:30.350
So my answer is,
I don't think there's one answer,

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:35.390
I think that as always you should do the
things that you love and are thrilled by.

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:39.473
But I think that when you do them,
you should do them with a perspective that

00:35:39.473 --> 00:35:44.150
you're going to have a lot of impact,
more than you think.

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:52.643
And that the flip side of getting to go
to this incredibly elite institution.

00:35:52.643 --> 00:35:56.523
I know people hate hearing that,
everyone would like to think, shucks,

00:35:56.523 --> 00:35:58.320
it's nothing special.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:35:59.620
It's very special,

00:35:59.620 --> 00:36:04.902
people here get an opportunity to have
impact that's absolutely disproportionate.

00:36:04.902 --> 00:36:08.215
And I think you should just be aware,
that when you do,

00:36:08.215 --> 00:36:11.830
you should take responsibility for
what you're causing.

00:36:12.980 --> 00:36:16.014
And that is,
I don't care what your job is,

00:36:16.014 --> 00:36:20.934
as long as you're doing that,
as long as you're thinking it through,

00:36:20.934 --> 00:36:25.618
and that your values are reasonable,
then you will do good things.

00:36:25.618 --> 00:36:31.614
And I think that is the responsibility
of getting all this special stuff,

00:36:31.614 --> 00:36:36.076
which I'm extremely grateful
to have gotten myself.

00:36:36.076 --> 00:36:40.953
It is a massive opportunity, and with
the massive opportunity, should come some

00:36:40.953 --> 00:36:46.070
sense of responsibility that you don't
just get to do this for yourself.

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:50.149
That if you're going to get
this special chance in society,

00:36:50.149 --> 00:36:55.311
that you should be thinking about what
that means for your relationship to

00:36:55.311 --> 00:37:00.487
all the other people in this society
who didn't get that special chance.

00:37:00.487 --> 00:37:05.306
And I think you don't do that, I think
that would be a profound mistake for

00:37:05.306 --> 00:37:08.349
someone.
&gt;&gt; We'll now open it up to questions

00:37:08.349 --> 00:37:09.470
from the audience.

00:37:09.470 --> 00:37:17.528
We've got some microphones.
&gt;&gt; Hi,

00:37:17.528 --> 00:37:20.537
Dr. Van Aken, I'm an MBA One here.

00:37:20.537 --> 00:37:23.560
Question on why impeachment?

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:25.807
Why not focus on the policy disagreements,

00:37:25.807 --> 00:37:28.184
raise those issues with
the American people?

00:37:28.184 --> 00:37:32.646
It strikes me from a rhetoric perspective
that saying the man's an existential

00:37:32.646 --> 00:37:37.242
threat, he should be removed from office
is to now action impact us a bit confusing

00:37:37.242 --> 00:37:44.570
and potentially dangerous, so thank you.
&gt;&gt; So, why impeachment?

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:47.830
Well, I do think the man's
an existential threat, for starters.

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:50.078
I do think that he's met the standard for
impeachment.

00:37:50.078 --> 00:37:54.420
And we're actually going to go through
that, I think, in public next week.

00:37:54.420 --> 00:38:02.046
But, I also think,
That there is a question in our society,

00:38:02.046 --> 00:38:07.051
really, about what we're doing and
whether we're in crisis.

00:38:07.051 --> 00:38:12.090
So I look at the impeachment question as,
are we in crisis?

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:16.999
And I talked to a friend of mine,
and I thought he said something,

00:38:16.999 --> 00:38:19.871
who runs a very large public company.

00:38:19.871 --> 00:38:25.616
And he said Tom, I have a 3% mortgage,
we have 4% unemployment,

00:38:25.616 --> 00:38:30.290
and the sock market is at a record,
how are we in crisis?

00:38:32.660 --> 00:38:38.751
And I said well the markets fluctuate,
economies change around.

00:38:38.751 --> 00:38:42.133
But if you look what was
going on politically,

00:38:42.133 --> 00:38:47.335
if you look at what's going on
internationally, if you look at what's

00:38:47.335 --> 00:38:53.144
going on in terms of long term decisions,
for the United States of America's,

00:38:53.144 --> 00:38:57.591
we are in a profound crisis that
people are not dealing with.

00:38:57.591 --> 00:39:00.370
And that is the point of impeachment.

00:39:00.370 --> 00:39:01.304
People are saying to me,

00:39:01.304 --> 00:39:03.849
actually I'm never going to use this
when they don't like a tax bill.

00:39:03.849 --> 00:39:06.078
This has nothing to do with a tax bill.

00:39:06.078 --> 00:39:11.307
We are in a profound crisis and the
question is when are Americans going to be

00:39:11.307 --> 00:39:16.622
able, are we going to as a group realize
this and understand what's at stake?

00:39:16.622 --> 00:39:20.894
So I actually think that going
after the individual bills,

00:39:20.894 --> 00:39:26.720
all of which I disagree with, every single
one of them, is the wrong way to do it.

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:30.112
That that is something where
you're really fighting in DC.

00:39:30.112 --> 00:39:32.532
I don't want to be fighting in DC.

00:39:32.532 --> 00:39:33.950
I don't think it's going to be effective.

00:39:33.950 --> 00:39:35.627
I think it's a waste of time and money.

00:39:35.627 --> 00:39:41.072
What I want to be doing is I want to
be talking to people in Des Moines,

00:39:41.072 --> 00:39:45.430
and Schenectady, and
Cleveland, and Dallas, and

00:39:45.430 --> 00:39:51.180
all across the country,
because those are the people who I trust.

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:56.717
I keep saying the solution for
us is going to be a broader democracy,

00:39:56.717 --> 00:40:02.369
not to go back to Washington DC to
beg people to be more thoughtful.

00:40:02.369 --> 00:40:04.358
&gt;&gt; To build on that question a little bit,

00:40:04.358 --> 00:40:06.830
you've fielded many
questions about whether or

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:10.991
not this impeachment campaign is setting
up a run for governor of California, and

00:40:10.991 --> 00:40:13.045
I'm not going to repeat that question.

00:40:13.045 --> 00:40:16.570
That talking about whom
you're fighting on behalf of.

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:20.802
I guess I was curious, what would you want
potential voters to know about you based

00:40:20.802 --> 00:40:22.995
on this impeachment campaign?
&gt;&gt; About me?

00:40:22.995 --> 00:40:24.967
&gt;&gt; Yes, because you're the one fighting

00:40:24.967 --> 00:40:27.080
for them.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well,

00:40:27.080 --> 00:40:28.090
I don't think it's about me.

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:30.580
Let's start by saying this
campaign is supposed to be about

00:40:30.580 --> 00:40:32.880
everybody putting their voice together.

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:37.630
The only thing I'd really like
people to understand is I think that

00:40:37.630 --> 00:40:40.880
most of the people who interview me
ask me what my hidden agenda is.

00:40:42.430 --> 00:40:47.350
And there's an assumption in there that no
one would do something because they just

00:40:47.350 --> 00:40:50.600
thought it was the right thing to do,
that there has to be something.

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:54.270
I remember when I started on climate,
people would say to me, but

00:40:54.270 --> 00:40:57.640
you're doing this to make money on
your clean energy investments, right?

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:01.940
And I'd be like, how does spending
tens of millions of dollars that has

00:41:01.940 --> 00:41:04.178
no response make people richer?

00:41:04.178 --> 00:41:08.472
It's-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:41:08.472 --> 00:41:10.058
&gt;&gt; But we actually made sure we made no

00:41:10.058 --> 00:41:12.906
clean energy investments except
through our foundation, so

00:41:12.906 --> 00:41:15.520
that we could just take
that off the table.

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:18.970
So when I think about this impeachment,
my whole point on this is

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:22.250
we're trying to do what's right,
because we think it's important.

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:25.710
That's what I want people to think,
that's all we want.

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:30.681
We want Americans to think
that there is a possibility to

00:41:30.681 --> 00:41:35.333
do what's right, and
that is what we are pushing for

00:41:35.333 --> 00:41:39.647
as hard as possible.
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name's Tyler Willerbram, MBA 1.

00:41:39.647 --> 00:41:44.595
And I'm curious to know, so, investing
all this money in technologies for

00:41:44.595 --> 00:41:49.470
sustainable energy, and trying to
influence policies to promote them.

00:41:49.470 --> 00:41:55.060
But people largely ignore the fact that if
you just got rid of fossil fuels today,

00:41:55.060 --> 00:41:59.160
you'd be having thousands of people
unemployed all of a sudden across the US.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:03.030
And largely this happened
in the coal industry, and

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:07.490
is definitely a factor that led to
the results of this past election.

00:42:07.490 --> 00:42:13.428
So to claim that we need to get to get to
sustainable energy as quickly as possible,

00:42:13.428 --> 00:42:16.612
but ignore this really
large fact of doing so

00:42:16.612 --> 00:42:22.313
could actually result in electing even
more in the same potential president.

00:42:22.313 --> 00:42:27.398
What's sort of your response to,
what are you doing to actually look at

00:42:27.398 --> 00:42:32.739
the American workers and people that
are going to be voting in these elections

00:42:32.739 --> 00:42:37.483
to ensure that if we do get to
a sustainable energy across the grid,

00:42:37.483 --> 00:42:42.770
how do we actually make sure that
we're not leaving other people behind?

00:42:44.590 --> 00:42:47.080
&gt;&gt; So, that is a great question.

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:51.180
And before I give my answer,
let me apologize for my answer,

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:57.040
because I completely disagree
with almost everything you said.

00:42:57.040 --> 00:42:58.960
We actually did a study on this.

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:03.410
So we did a study what happens to
employment if we move to clean energy.

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.578
We can't move to clean energy tomorrow.

00:43:05.578 --> 00:43:09.750
It's always going to
be a question of pace.

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:12.730
But we did a study with Hank Paulson,

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:17.110
the Republican Secretary of
the Treasury under George W Bush, and

00:43:17.110 --> 00:43:21.740
Mike Bloomberg, who's been a Democrat, a
Republican, and is sort of an independent,

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.740
I guess, to make sure that it
wasn't a partisan's fight.

00:43:24.740 --> 00:43:29.830
And it basically showed, that in fact, if
we moved to clean energy, it creates more

00:43:29.830 --> 00:43:35.940
jobs, that we will have higher paying
jobs, the United States will grow faster.

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:41.080
And this isn't taking into account
the cost of having the climate change,

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:44.920
and the kinds of disasters that
are going to cost the United States $300

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:45.770
billion this year.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:52.430
But there are a couple true points in
what you're saying that are absolutely

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.680
important politically, and I agree with.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:43:57.550
One of them has to do with
what are you going to do with.

00:43:57.550 --> 00:43:59.560
It's true there will be more jobs, and

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:03.210
it's true the jobs will be better paying,
but not for the same people.

00:44:03.210 --> 00:44:05.940
So the question is,
what are you going to do?

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:11.190
My roommate, my ex-roommate lives in
the poorest county in West Virginia.

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:13.881
His brother-in-law is
an unemployed coal miner, and

00:44:13.881 --> 00:44:15.834
his cousin is an unemployed coal miner.

00:44:15.834 --> 00:44:17.504
So I went down to see him,

00:44:17.504 --> 00:44:22.480
he's a retired career army officer
with some health problems.

00:44:22.480 --> 00:44:24.800
And I went down, and he was telling me,

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:27.040
if you're a coal miner today,
you make 82 bucks an hour.

00:44:28.070 --> 00:44:33.011
Which, in the poorest county in West
Virginia, is an unbelievable amount of

00:44:33.011 --> 00:44:36.670
money, and the next stop is
probably 25 bucks an hour.

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:40.670
So coal mining is not
the world's greatest job.

00:44:40.670 --> 00:44:43.930
It's a really well paid
job with good benefits.

00:44:43.930 --> 00:44:45.900
And that's really what
we're talking about.

00:44:45.900 --> 00:44:50.499
We're talking about what are we going
to do for the people in our society who

00:44:50.499 --> 00:44:55.689
think, look, there are 50,000 coal
miners in the United States of America.

00:44:55.689 --> 00:44:59.123
It's a very, very, very,
very small number of people,

00:44:59.123 --> 00:45:03.903
but there's a broader question here,
and that's what Trump was referring to.

00:45:03.903 --> 00:45:07.538
There are a group of people in the United
States of America who do not live in

00:45:07.538 --> 00:45:08.841
Palo Alto, California.

00:45:08.841 --> 00:45:11.158
They do not live in New York City.

00:45:11.158 --> 00:45:16.780
They do not live in the parts of
the country that are seeing real growth.

00:45:16.780 --> 00:45:20.146
They are not connected to
the information technology revolution.

00:45:20.146 --> 00:45:24.210
They are not connected to the positive
aspects of globalization.

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:27.300
And they can see the climate changing,
but for

00:45:27.300 --> 00:45:32.800
them, that is not the immediate
problem they're dealing with.

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:35.240
That's just something that
may put them out of work.

00:45:35.240 --> 00:45:38.505
So they feel completely
scared by the fact,

00:45:38.505 --> 00:45:41.910
all of the fact that
the world is changing.

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:46.618
That people in Palo Alto go like, whoa,
just killed it on my latest investment.

00:45:46.618 --> 00:45:50.190
They're going, whoa, the world is
changing, and they just closed the plant.

00:45:50.190 --> 00:45:54.210
And there's only one plant, or
they just closed the coal mine, and

00:45:54.210 --> 00:45:55.550
now what do we do?

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:58.945
But now we're going to get less than half
the dollars per hour that we thought we

00:45:58.945 --> 00:46:01.140
were going to get or that we were getting.

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:05.110
So the question to me is not
the macro question you asked,

00:46:05.110 --> 00:46:06.980
but the micro question.

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:10.850
What do we say to the people
in rural America or

00:46:10.850 --> 00:46:14.850
poor urban America who are not
connected to the growth for

00:46:14.850 --> 00:46:19.910
whom the rapid pace of change in
our society is scary as anything?

00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:24.290
And that is what Trump was dealing with,
that fear, that uncertainty, and

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:28.370
that sense that no one was listening.

00:46:28.370 --> 00:46:31.010
And I think his answer
was a dishonest one.

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:36.970
And I think it was absolutely, we're
not going back to a lot of coal miners.

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:40.660
Even if we dug a ton more coal,
it's all mechanized.

00:46:40.660 --> 00:46:45.990
No one is going into a coal face with
a pick and shovel anymore, period.

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:50.212
But it was a perfect symbol for
going back to a time when being

00:46:50.212 --> 00:46:54.773
a hard working American who was
willing to go to work every day and

00:46:54.773 --> 00:47:00.286
kill yourself meant you got a middle
class job, including in rural America.

00:47:00.286 --> 00:47:04.885
So the question is when we talk to those
people who are hard working and scared,

00:47:04.885 --> 00:47:07.811
how are we actually,
not how are we going to fake it,

00:47:07.811 --> 00:47:10.200
how are we actually going to include them?

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:12.730
And to me, the question is two-fold.

00:47:12.730 --> 00:47:15.580
One is, we need to invest in
the people of the United States.

00:47:16.620 --> 00:47:18.720
That means education, training.

00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:23.580
We need to connect them to this world
that you guys are in right here in this

00:47:23.580 --> 00:47:24.545
auditorium.

00:47:24.545 --> 00:47:27.091
If you don't have
broadband in the community,

00:47:27.091 --> 00:47:29.200
you cannot compete in the first world.

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:34.160
When I was in West Virginia,
the roads were bad, literally.

00:47:35.750 --> 00:47:38.010
So the question is,
if you're in that community,

00:47:38.010 --> 00:47:43.340
how can you possibly compete in this
world or even be part of this world?

00:47:43.340 --> 00:47:46.090
And this includes rural California.

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:49.620
If you're not connected real-time,
it can't happen.

00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:53.470
Secondly, if we're not investing in
those people, we lose, they lose,

00:47:53.470 --> 00:47:54.880
the country loses.

00:47:54.880 --> 00:47:59.650
The idea that we're going to
cut education, training,

00:47:59.650 --> 00:48:04.410
healthcare, food for
poor kids from the budget is insane.

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:08.550
It's morally wrong, but it's also crazy.

00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:11.520
This country will do well
if Americans do well.

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:14.980
We should be taking our money and
investing in those people.

00:48:14.980 --> 00:48:18.050
So that they think I'm going
to be connected to the world,

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:19.430
I'm going to be trained for the world.

00:48:19.430 --> 00:48:24.900
I'm going to participate in this world,
in the 21st century, so that every change

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:29.550
doesn't actually push me further away
from what's going on in the real world.

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:36.300
Because if we were in rural Wisconsin,
and there was one plant in our town,

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:40.920
and that plant closed, we wouldn't
know what to do either, except leave.

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:44.500
That's what's going on in this world.

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:50.350
We need to invest and frankly, clean
energy, it means rebuilding this country,

00:48:50.350 --> 00:48:56.420
and it actually means this country
with millions of net new jobs.

00:48:56.420 --> 00:48:57.850
We have to do it.

00:48:57.850 --> 00:49:04.210
It's a way for us in the short-term to get
every community rural, poor urban people.

00:49:04.210 --> 00:49:07.790
People in rural America,
we have to do it every where.

00:49:07.790 --> 00:49:08.920
It's good jobs every where.

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:12.240
The question is,
what do the jobs pay and who gets them?

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:18.150
The last thing I'll say is this, I was
in Cleveland talking to some inner city

00:49:18.150 --> 00:49:23.090
ministers about clean energy jobs,
and they were excited,

00:49:23.090 --> 00:49:28.810
I promise you, at the idea that we
could bring thousands of jobs to urban,

00:49:28.810 --> 00:49:34.369
poor Cleveland with high unemployment for
their people, in their communities.

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:37.714
It's true in rural Wisconsin too.

00:49:37.714 --> 00:49:42.441
So when I hear it I know he's talking,
the president was dealing with

00:49:42.441 --> 00:49:47.088
a real emotional problem that is
absolutely felt in this country but

00:49:47.088 --> 00:49:49.392
he wasn't giving a fair answer.

00:49:49.392 --> 00:49:53.506
And the answer I'm giving about invest
in Americans, rebuild the country and

00:49:53.506 --> 00:49:57.743
connect us all together, is actually the
way that somebody living in a rural part

00:49:57.743 --> 00:50:01.290
of this country can connect
with you guys in this room.

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:04.650
Or you can choose to live in
a rural part of California and

00:50:04.650 --> 00:50:06.930
still connect with the fastest growing,

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:12.370
most vibrant companies in the world.
&gt;&gt; We are almost out of time,

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:15.430
so I'm just going to take the final
question, so we can wrap up, so

00:50:15.430 --> 00:50:18.210
officially according to the clock.

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:22.910
But the holidays are right around the
corner, and I've read that you are known

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:27.710
for requiring guests at your annual
Christmas lunch to bring a poem to read.

00:50:28.820 --> 00:50:30.111
What's the best poem you've heard?

00:50:33.932 --> 00:50:42.560
Well, I would say there are some
great American poets right now.

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:46.420
The two that I would recommend to you if
you're not reading your poetry every night

00:50:46.420 --> 00:50:51.760
before bed, Billy Collins is hilarious and

00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:57.160
really profound and
Mary Oliver is fantastic and super fun.

00:50:58.210 --> 00:51:03.311
And wow, if you read just those two poets,

00:51:03.311 --> 00:51:07.771
they are, I promise you,

00:51:07.771 --> 00:51:12.569
you will laugh really hard and
you will walk away happier, and

00:51:12.569 --> 00:51:18.810
with a sense of some, the world being
a place where we can all make it better.

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:20.360
We can have a positive impact.

00:51:20.360 --> 00:51:22.980
And that's what we're all trying,
the one thing I would say to you guys is,

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:28.120
look, having a meaningful life
is what you should be going for.

00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:29.600
That is what will make you happy.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:35.080
If you sit there and
think I'm having a positive impact.

00:51:35.080 --> 00:51:37.220
I'm having a meaningful life.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:40.210
That is what you will
want when you're my age.

00:51:40.210 --> 00:51:43.340
And so when people say you're really
trying to do this to make more money,

00:51:43.340 --> 00:51:46.700
I'm like no, selfishly I'm trying
to have a positive impact.

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:48.232
I'm not trying to say it's not selfish.

00:51:48.232 --> 00:51:52.260
But that's what, I promise you guys,
if you're doing that,

00:51:52.260 --> 00:51:57.200
you will find great joy in that, and
that's what I've observed in myself and

00:51:57.200 --> 00:51:59.961
all the people who I meet.
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen,

00:51:59.961 --> 00:52:05.503
please join me in thanking Tom.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:52:05.503 --> 00:52:09.930
[MUSIC]

